course: politics
lesson: 29
title: Taiwan, China and the word «tensions»
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «Tensions» means a situation is tight and worried, like a rubber band pulled hard but not snapped. There is no war between China and Taiwan; there are warnings, angry words and shows of strength.
explain: After a civil war in 1949, the Communist Party set up the People's Republic of China on the mainland, and the losing government moved to the island of Taiwan, keeping the old name, the Republic of China. Taiwan later became a democracy.
explain: Today Taiwan's 23 million people elect their own president and have their own army, money and passports. China's government says Taiwan is part of China and must one day be joined, and has not ruled out force; polls show most people in Taiwan want to keep things as they are.
explain: Two kids holding two different maps can both be holding real maps: one shows who actually runs Taiwan, the other what China and some others officially say. Neither tells the whole story alone.
explain: Words from the scenes: «sweet as» (New Zealand) = fine, «eh» = right?; «gurt» (Bristol) = very big, «innit» = isn't it; «rizz» = charm, «fr» = for real; «lah» and «can or not» (Singapore) = a friendly tag and «is it possible?».
fact: «Tensions» means a tight, worried situation where a fight could happen; there is no war between China and Taiwan.
fact: Taiwan is an island of about 23 million people with its own elected president, army, money and passports.
fact: China, officially the People's Republic of China, says Taiwan is part of China and must one day be joined, and has not ruled out using force.
fact: Only about a dozen countries officially recognise Taiwan as a country, though many more trade and talk with it unofficially.
fact: In 1949, after a civil war, the Communist Party set up the People's Republic of China on the mainland, and the losing side moved to Taiwan, keeping the name Republic of China.
fact: News reports said China's army held large exercises around Taiwan several times, including in 2022, 2024 and 2025; China called them a warning against Taiwan breaking away, and Taiwan called them threatening.
fact: The US does not officially recognise Taiwan as a country, but a US law says it will help Taiwan defend itself and it sells Taiwan weapons; for years it has not said clearly whether it would fight for Taiwan.
fact: Taiwan makes most of the world's most advanced computer chips, used in phones, cars and games consoles.
fact: At the Olympics Taiwan's teams compete as «Chinese Taipei» with a special flag, a compromise that lets its athletes take part.
fact: Polls in Taiwan have shown most people want to keep things as they are, neither declaring full independence nor joining China.
fact: Maps of disputed places are drawn differently depending on who makes them, and some companies change their maps for different countries.
fact: China and Taiwan trade a lot with each other, and diplomats work every day to keep the peace.
exercise: Is there a war between China and Taiwan? | No, there are tensions but no war | Yes, it started last year, in spring | Yes, and it ended in 2020 with a deal | No, because they are very far apart
exercise: What does «tensions» mean in the news? | A tight, worried situation where a fight could happen | A war that has already started | A signed peace treaty | A sports competition
exercise: About how many people live in Taiwan? | About 23 million | About 23 thousand | About 2 billion | About 230
exercise: What does China's government say about Taiwan? | It is part of China and must one day be joined | It is part of Japan | It is a separate country China fully recognises | It is a city in the middle of China
exercise: Which of these does Taiwan have of its own? | An elected president, an army, money and passports | Nothing; it is run day to day from Beijing | Only a football team | Only a flag for the Olympics
exercise: About how many countries officially recognise Taiwan as a country? | About a dozen | Every country on Earth | None at all, not one | About 190 of them
exercise: Why is Taiwan called «Republic of China» and China «People's Republic of China»? | After the 1949 civil war the losing side moved to Taiwan and kept the old name | They are two names for one city | A map maker mixed them up hundreds of years ago, and nobody ever bothered to put the names back | Taiwan bought the name in a sale
exercise: What were the large drills China held around Taiwan? | Military practice with ships and planes, meant as a warning | A real war | A boat race | A fishing festival
exercise: Why does the US president not say clearly what America would do if China attacked Taiwan? | The US has kept it unclear on purpose to keep both sides guessing | The US has promised never to help Taiwan in any way, whatever happens there in the future | The US has promised to attack China first | The president was not asked properly
exercise: Why is Ethan's new phone linked to Taiwan? | Taiwan makes most of the world's most advanced chips | Phones are banned in Taiwan | Taiwan invented the telephone | All phone apps are written in Taiwan
exercise: Why do Taiwan's athletes walk in as «Chinese Taipei»? | It is a compromise that lets them compete despite the dispute | They chose it because they like it best | Taipei is the name of a sport | They come from a different island called Taipei, which is next to Taiwan and has its own team
exercise: What do polls say most people in Taiwan want? | To keep things as they are | To join China at once | To declare full independence today | To move to Australia
exercise: Why do Dylan's game map and his friend's school map show Taiwan in different colours? | Maps of disputed places differ depending on who makes them | One of the maps is upside down | Taiwan changes colour each season | Game maps are always the official truth
exercise: What is a video called «World War 3 is coming» most likely trying to do? | Grab clicks with fear | Give calm, checked facts | Report a war that has started | Teach maths to small children
exercise: What do China and Taiwan do a lot of that helps keep the peace? | Trade with each other | Play football every week | Share one army between them | Swap presidents each year
test: Mia in Auckland says: «sweet as, the radio says tensions — so they're at war, eh?» What is right? | Tensions means a tight, worried situation; there is no war | Yes, tensions is another word for war | Tensions means a big party | Tensions only happen in sport
test: Kai's friends shout «yes, Taiwan's a country!» and «no, it's China!» Which answer is fair? | Taiwan runs itself, China says it is part of China, and few countries officially recognise it | Everyone agrees Taiwan is part of Japan | Everyone in the world agrees on the answer, so whichever friend shouts loudest must be the one who is right | Taiwan does not exist
test: What would happen to phones and games consoles if Taiwan's chip factories stopped? | Making them would get much harder, because Taiwan makes most advanced chips | Nothing, because chips come from the Moon | Phones would work better than before, because the chips from other places are faster and cheaper | Only phones sold in Taiwan would stop
test: Sam's mate in Bristol says: «gurt big warships all round Taiwan — war's on then, innit?» What is the careful reading? | China held large drills as a warning; a drill is not a war | Yes, the drills are the war | The ships are on a cruise holiday | It is a film, not the news
test: Noah in Denver says: «why won't the president just say? zero rizz, fr.» Why might he stay vague on purpose? | Keeping both sides guessing is meant to stop anyone risking a war | He forgot what the question was | The US has no view about Taiwan at all and has never once made any law about it or sold it anything | It is illegal to talk about Taiwan
test: Zara's friend from Taiwan sighs at «Chinese Taipei». Which statement is fair? | Some say the name lets athletes compete; others say Taiwan should use its own name | Everyone in Taiwan loves the name and asked for it, because it sounds better than any other name | Taiwan is not allowed at the Olympics | Taipei is a different country from Taiwan
test: Grace's friend in Glasgow says: «we don't want war, and we don't want to be told what to do.» What fits the polls? | Most people in Taiwan want to keep things as they are | Most people in Taiwan want war with China | Most want to join China tomorrow | Most want to move to Scotland
test: Dylan's game shows Taiwan the same colour as China; the school map shows its own colour. Who is wrong? | Neither tells the whole story: one follows official claims, the other who runs it | The game, because games are always wrong | The school map, because schools are always wrong and game makers check every border with the UN first | Both, because Taiwan is in Africa
test: Hannah's brother in Leeds says: «World War 3 next week, we're all done for, lol.» What is the calm truth? | No war has happened; fear videos grab clicks, and diplomats work to keep the peace | War definitely starts next week, because a video with a lot of views says so and videos are checked | China and Taiwan never talk or trade | Videos with scary titles are always true
test: A friend from Singapore says: «China and Taiwan, both called China, so confusing lah — can explain or not?» What explains it? | After the 1949 civil war the losing side moved to Taiwan and kept the old name | They are the same island | Taiwan named itself after a restaurant in Beijing that its first president liked to eat at every week | A map maker swapped the names
test: Which statement fairly gives both sides' views of the drills? | China says they warn against Taiwan breaking away; Taiwan calls them threatening | Both say the drills were a party | Both say there were no drills, and that the ships in the photos were only fishing boats far out at sea | Taiwan held the drills around China
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present relationship between China and Taiwan? | It is one of tension, without war | It is one of open warfare on land and sea | It is one of formal union under a single government | It is one of total isolation without trade
exam: Identify the event of 1949 from which the two designations «Republic of China» and «People's Republic of China» derive. | The conclusion of a civil war, after which the losing government withdrew to Taiwan | The signing of the Charter of the United Nations, which assigned a separate designation to each government | The founding of the Olympic Games | The invention of the computer chip
exam: State the designation under which athletes from Taiwan compete at the Olympic Games. | Chinese Taipei | Republic of Taiwan | United Formosa Team | People's Republic of Taipei
exam: Select the response consistent with the established policy of the United States in respect of Taiwan. | It does not formally recognise Taiwan as a state, yet by law assists its defence and declines to state whether it would fight | It formally recognises Taiwan as its fifty-first state | It has declared that it shall never sell any equipment to Taiwan, and has withdrawn all assistance of every kind since the year 1949 | It has undertaken in writing to transfer Taiwan to China
